mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of data viz & infographics in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nVolatile figures — attribute, mark **verify-quarterly**. The craft principles (title = takeaway; honest scales;\ndata-ink; accessibility) are stable and well-established (Tufte, storytelling-with-data, WCAG).\n\n## The job: make one insight impossible to miss\n- A visualization doesn't add information — it makes the insight that was always in the numbers **impossible to\n  miss.** Most charts fail by burying the takeaway or quietly distorting it.\n- **The title is the takeaway, not a label.** \"Revenue grew 28% YoY\" beats \"Revenue by Quarter\"; \"Global Sales\n  Declined 5% in Q4\" beats \"Quarterly Sales.\" A reader should get the point in ~3 seconds without decoding.\n- **One chart, one message.** Limit the big idea to a central theme (~2–3 concepts max); if you're showing three\n  things, make three visuals.\n\n## Honest scales (the integrity spine — stable)\n- **The #1 chart crime is a truncated y-axis** (Tufte's \"lie factor\"): a y-axis starting at $90M turns $95M→$100M\n  (a real 5.3% rise) into a bar that looks ~2× taller. **Bar/column charts must start at zero.** Line charts may\n  use a non-zero baseline only if clearly labeled and appropriate to the range.\n- **Avoid 3D, skewed perspective, area/bubble distortion, dual axes, inconsistent panel scales, cherry-picked\n  ranges** — all manufacture a false read.\n- **Context prevents misreads:** per the University of Utah Visualization Design Lab, missing contextual info\n  drives misleading interpretations in up to ~84% of cases (attribute) — add the denominator/caveat.\n- **Honest viz is strategy, not just ethics:** misleading charts get called out and fact-checked; the credibility\n  hit outweighs the punchy distortion.\n\n## Reduce to the signal (data-ink ratio)\nEvery element should show data or essential context; everything else is noise. Strip 3D, drop shadows, gradients,\ndecorative backgrounds, and unnecessary gridlines (they make charts harder to read). **Direct-label** instead of\nleaning on a busy legend; drop axis labels the title already implies; one color + an accent to highlight the key\nvalue. **Default to a bar chart when unsure** (versatile, rarely misleads). Sometimes the honest answer is **not a\nchart** — for one or two numbers, a big stat or a small table is clearer.\n\n## Accessibility (craft, not an afterthought)\n~8% of men / ~0.5% of women have color vision deficiency — **never rely on color alone**; use redundant encoding\n(labels, shapes, arrows ↑/↓, +/−, patterns). Meet **WCAG contrast** (4.5:1 normal text / 3:1 large). **Alt text\nstates the takeaway/trend** (formula: \"chart type of data where takeaway\"), not every value; pair complex charts\nwith a data table.\n\n## House metrics\nSaves/shares (data viz over-indexes on saves) + AI-citation share (well-sourced data is highly citable) + clicks\n— read natively; never fabricate a number or a source.\n"
